==============================================================================
PART 1 -- the natural 3-Gaussian candidate:  P = (1+W)(Wbar - X^2)
==============================================================================
  In 3 real Gaussians we cannot use a complex conjugate pair for W and still have
  a real X left over unless we spend 2 on W.  Write W = u1 + i u2 with
  u_i ~ N(0,1/2); then (1+W)(Wbar - X^2) has COMPLEX coefficients.  Its real and
  imaginary parts are each real polynomials in (u1,u2,X), so we test those.

  E[|Z|^{2k}] = k!   but   E[X^{2k}] = (2k-1)!!   -- the factorials that made the
  alternating binomial sum collapse are GONE.  Checking whether it still works:

    m=1:  real-square analogue =            0    complex |Z|^2 version = 0
    m=2:  real-square analogue =            1    complex |Z|^2 version = 0
    m=3:  real-square analogue =            0    complex |Z|^2 version = 0
    m=4:  real-square analogue =            9    complex |Z|^2 version = 0
    m=5:  real-square analogue =            0    complex |Z|^2 version = 0
    m=6:  real-square analogue =          225    complex |Z|^2 version = 0
    m=7:  real-square analogue =            0    complex |Z|^2 version = 0
  => the real-square substitution does NOT vanish; the mechanism is specific to
     the COMPLEX modulus, whose moments k! are exactly what cancel C(m,k).

==============================================================================
PART 2 -- broad search over 3 real Gaussians
==============================================================================
  deg<=3, support<=4, coeffs(-1, 1): 70490 polys tested, 0 counterexamples
  deg<=3, support<=5, coeffs(-1, 1): 442586 polys tested, 0 counterexamples
  deg<=2, support<=6, coeffs(-1, 1): 12258 polys tested, 0 counterexamples

==============================================================================
PART 3 -- CONTROL: the same search at n = 4 real Gaussians must FIND something
==============================================================================
  n=4, deg<=3, support<=4, unit coeffs: 792200 tested, 0 counterexamples
  NOTE: none found in REAL coordinates at this support size, even though the
  complex-coordinate 4-term example exists -- the real expansion of
  (1+W)(Wbar-|Z|^2) has more terms and non-unit coefficients, so it lies
  OUTSIDE this search box.  The box is the limitation, not the mathematics.

SUMMARY
  The mechanism needs E[|Z|^{2k}] = k! exactly -- that is what cancels the binomial
  coefficient and turns the sum into sum_j (-1)^j C(m,j).  A real square gives
  (2k-1)!! instead and the collapse fails.  No 3-real-Gaussian counterexample was
  found in the searched boxes; n >= 4 SURVIVES as the record, but sharpness is
  NOT proved -- these are bounded searches over unit coefficients only.
